// Agent run environment, usually "production" or "preview". Defaults to "production".
environment?: string,
// Start of a custom time range: ISO 8601, Unix seconds, Unix milliseconds, "now", or a relative duration such as "12h" (12 hours before now; units s, m, h, d, w). Must be used with to.
from?: string,
// Maximum length for individual string fields in the returned trace. Defaults to 8000; use 0 to disable truncation.
maxFieldLength?: number,
// Preset time range to query. Ignored when both from and to are provided. When period, from, and to are all omitted, no range is sent and the Agent Runs API applies its own default range.
period?: "5m" | "15m" | "1h" | "6h" | "12h" | "1d" | "3d" | "7d" | "14d" | "30d" | "90d",
// The ID or slug of the project.
// Project IDs start with "prj_".
// If you do not know the project ID or slug, it can be found through these mechanism:
// - Read the file .vercel/project.json if it exists and extract the projectId
// - Use the `list_projects` tool
projectId: string,
// ID of the Agent Run to inspect. Get it from list_agent_runs for the same project.
runId: string,
// The ID or slug of the team that owns the resource.
// Team IDs start with "team_".
// If you do not know the team ID or slug, it can be found through these mechanism:
// - Read the file .vercel/project.json if it exists and extract the orgId
// - Use the `list_teams` tool
teamId: string,
// End of a custom time range: ISO 8601, Unix seconds, Unix milliseconds, "now", or a relative duration such as "1h" (1 hour before now; units s, m, h, d, w). Must be used with from.
to?: string,
// Copy cursor from later to fetch the next page, with its traceStreamId.
traceAfterCursor?: string, // minLength: 1
// Copy beforeChunk from earlier to fetch the previous page, with its traceStreamId.
traceBeforeChunk?: integer, // maximum: 9007199254740991, exclusiveMinimum: 0
// Copy streamId from the trace payload’s later or earlier object. Requires exactly one pagination cursor.
traceStreamId?: string, // minLength: 1
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Use this when asking which models, model labs, apps, or inference providers are most used on Vercel AI Gateway. models and labs return each name's percentage share of one metric on one day, highest first: requests on the most recent day unless metric, modality, or date say otherwise. apps and providers return two top-10 lists of names, one ranked by token volume and one by spend, told apart by ranked_by. When more rows remain, pass the returned nextOffset as offset. The data is open and anonymized (CC BY 4.0) and covers all of AI Gateway, not the user's account; use get_ai_gateway_usage for that. App names are supplied by third parties.

```ts
mcp__Vercel__get_ai_gateway_leaderboard(args: {
// Which ranking to return.
dataset: "models" | "labs" | "apps" | "providers",
// The day to return for models and labs, as YYYY-MM-DD. Defaults to the most recent day. Ignored for apps and providers.
date?: string, // pattern: /^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}$/
// Maximum rows to return. Defaults to 25.
limit?: integer, // minimum: 1, maximum: 100
// What models and labs are ranked by. Defaults to requests. Ignored for apps and providers.
metric?: "requests" | "tokens" | "spend" | "imageCount" | "videoCount",
// Only count text, image, or video usage, for models and labs. Defaults to all. A metric the modality does not have, such as tokens for video, returns no rows. Ignored for apps and providers.
modality?: "all" | "text" | "image" | "video",
// Rows to skip. Pass the nextOffset from the previous call to get the next page.
offset?: integer, // minimum: 0, maximum: 9007199254740991
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

