Resolve a calendar/meeting URL the user pasted (a Google Calendar event link, or a Meet/Zoom conference link) to the matching calendar event — title, time, attendees, conference URL, and its calendar_id. Use this whenever the user pastes such a link instead of guessing at its contents. To then pull the event again later, use get_calendar_event with the returned calendar_id. Every timestamp in the response is UTC (ISO 8601, ending in 'Z'). Convert times to the user's local time zone before presenting them — do not show the raw UTC time to the user.

```ts
mcp__Wispr_Flow__resolve_calendar_link(args: {
// The pasted calendar or conference URL.
url: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Get the transcript, notes, or summary of a Wispr Flow meeting from a share link (https://notes.wisprflow.ai/shared/… or its bare slug). Use this rather than fetching the page, which does not include the transcript. Returns the title, Flow Summary (whose next-steps section holds the meeting's action items), owner, and the caller's role. The owner also receives their user-authored My Notes. Pass view_transcript={} when the user wants the transcript: a bounded range is returned only when the caller owns the note or is an explicitly invited recipient AND the owner's organization shares transcripts. Domain/team access, anyone-with-link access, or a notes-only organization omits transcript and returns a transcript_access explanation when requested. When truncated, use the continuation offset in the marker to request the next range. Errors if the link is not a share link or no shared note is accessible for it. Every timestamp in the response is UTC (ISO 8601, ending in 'Z'). Convert times to the user's local time zone before presenting them — do not show the raw UTC time to the user.

```ts
mcp__Wispr_Flow__resolve_share_link(args: {
// The pasted share link (or its bare slug).
url: string,
// Request a bounded character range of the normalized plaintext transcript. Omit this object to omit the transcript. If the response is truncated, call the tool again with start_char set to the exact continuation offset in the appended marker.
view_transcript?: {
// Maximum transcript characters to return (default 12000, server-enforced max 40000).
char_limit?: integer, // default: 12000, minimum: 1, maximum: 40000
// Zero-based character offset into the normalized plaintext transcript (default 0).
start_char?: integer, // default: 0, minimum: 0
},
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Search or list the user's Google Calendar events. Use to find meetings by who's on them (attendee_emails — the precise way to answer 'meetings with alex@acme.com', or 'with both alex and bob' via attendee_match=all), by keyword in the title/description, or within a date window. For a whole local calendar day (including earlier events), use since and until. Returns each event's title, time, conference URL, and an attendee preview (up to 5 structured attendees plus attendee_count). Use get_calendar_event for the complete attendee list. Note: these are calendar events, not the recorded meeting notes. With no until, far-future events come first; pass until (for example, now) to list past events. Results are paginated, latest start time first. If the response's has_more is true there are more matches: call again with cursor set to the returned next_cursor (copied verbatim) to continue. When more remain, a 'more' field spells out how many and how to continue. To answer 'all'/'every'/'ever' questions, keep paging until has_more is false. Paging is capped at 1000 results per query; truncated=true means more matches exist beyond the cap — narrow the query. Every timestamp in the response is UTC (ISO 8601, ending in 'Z'). Convert times to the user's local time zone before presenting them — do not show the raw UTC time to the user.

```ts
mcp__Wispr_Flow__search_calendar_events(args: {
// Filter by attendee email(s), exact addresses (case-insensitive). One email is the common case; pass several with attendee_match to require all or any of them. Omit to not filter by attendee.
attendee_emails?: string[],
// How to combine multiple attendee_emails: 'any' (default) matches anyone in the list; 'all' matches only items where every listed attendee was present. Ignored for a single email.
attendee_match?: "any" | "all",
// Pagination cursor: the next_cursor from a previous response. Omit for the first page.
cursor?: string,
// Page size (default 25, max 200).
limit?: integer, // minimum: 1
// Keyword to match against event title/description.
query?: string,
// Only events starting at or after this ISO datetime with offset/Z.
since?: string,
// Only events starting strictly before this ISO datetime with offset/Z. Use the next local midnight for a day filter.
until?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Search or list meetings captured by Wispr Flow Meeting Recorder. Use when the user asks about their meetings, calls, standups, 1:1s, syncs, or anything discussed in a recorded conversation. Returns the most recently modified meetings when no query is given. For meetings on a particular day, pass since/until (when the meeting happened, half-open). Filter by who was there with attendee_emails (matched against the meeting's linked calendar event) — the precise way to answer 'my meetings with alex@acme.com'. Each result lists up to 5 attendees (more are summarized — call get_meeting for the full list), start/end times, and a has_transcript flag; when true, follow up with get_meeting and view_transcript={} and answer from the transcript rather than the lossy notes summary. Available meetings include the user's own meetings, meetings shared with them as an email recipient, and meetings in enabled shared folders they can access. Shared meetings expose the summary and any transcript permitted by sharing policy; the owner's private notes, tasks and contact details remain withheld. Meetings accessible only through domain or anyone-with-link sharing require the note URL and resolve_share_link. Results with shared=true are another person's notes (owner.name): attribute their content and tasks to the people in them, never to the user, unless the user is named as the one who said or committed to it. folders lists the Wispr Flow folders the meeting is filed in that the user can see (their own and ones shared with them), as {id, name} sorted by name; omitted when it is in none. share_link is the meeting's notes page in Wispr Flow. Give it to the user whenever they ask for a link. Who else can open it depends on that meeting's sharing setting, and this server cannot read or change that setting. If the user wants to change who a link works for, tell them to open the Wispr Flow app, go to the Notetaker tab, click the menu to the right of the meeting, and click Share — that opens the sharing settings for that meeting. A meeting with no share_link has no notes page yet — say so rather than guessing a URL. Results are paginated, most recently modified first. If the response's has_more is true there are more matches: call again with cursor set to the returned next_cursor (copied verbatim) to continue. When more remain, a 'more' field says how many, exactly up to 100 and as '100+' beyond that, and how to continue. To answer 'all'/'every'/'ever' questions, keep paging until has_more is false. Paging is capped at 1000 results per query; truncated=true means more matches exist beyond the cap — narrow the query. Every timestamp in the response is UTC (ISO 8601, ending in 'Z'). Convert times to the user's local time zone before presenting them — do not show the raw UTC time to the user.

```ts
mcp__Wispr_Flow__search_meetings(args: {
// Filter by attendee email(s), exact addresses (case-insensitive). One email is the common case; pass several with attendee_match to require all or any of them. Omit to not filter by attendee.
attendee_emails?: string[],
// How to combine multiple attendee_emails: 'any' (default) matches anyone in the list; 'all' matches only items where every listed attendee was present. Ignored for a single email.
attendee_match?: "any" | "all",
// Filter by person: contact_id values from get_meeting_participants_enriched (participants or mentioned_contacts). Matches your own meetings that invited, heard or mentioned any of them, including people only talked about. Omit to not filter by person.
contact_ids?: string[], // maxItems: 20
// Pagination cursor: the next_cursor from a previous response. Omit for the first page.
cursor?: string,
// Which field to search: title, content, or both.
field?: "title" | "content" | "both",
// Page size (default 25, max 200).
limit?: integer, // minimum: 1
// Keyword matching title, summary, owned notes and speaker names, not transcript. Shared notes match title, summary and speaker names only. Omit to browse the date window or recent meetings.
query?: string,
// Only meetings starting at or after this ISO datetime with offset/Z (calendar start, else stored creation time).
since?: string,
// Result order. 'modified' (default) lists by last edit, newest first, with cursor paging. 'start' lists meetings that have begun by when each happened, newest first, in one page with no cursor; pass until to include upcoming ones, and narrow with since and until. Use 'start' for the user's most recent or latest meetings.
sort?: "modified" | "start",
// Only meetings starting strictly before this ISO datetime with offset/Z. Use the next local midnight for a day filter.
until?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Search or list the user's notes (Wispr Flow scratchpad). Use whenever the user asks about their notes, scratchpad, jottings, or anything they've written down. Returns the most recently modified notes when no query is given; case-insensitive substring match otherwise. Results are paginated, most recently modified first. If the response's has_more is true there are more matches: call again with cursor set to the returned next_cursor (copied verbatim) to continue. When more remain, a 'more' field spells out how many and how to continue. To answer 'all'/'every'/'ever' questions, keep paging until has_more is false. Paging is capped at 1000 results per query; truncated=true means more matches exist beyond the cap — narrow the query.

```ts
mcp__Wispr_Flow__search_scratchpad_notes(args: {
// Pagination cursor: the next_cursor from a previous response. Omit for the first page.
cursor?: string,
// Which field to search: title, content, or both.
field?: "title" | "content" | "both",
// Page size (default 25, max 200).
limit?: integer, // minimum: 1
// Search term to match against notes. Omit to list all notes.
query?: string,
// Only notes modified at or after this ISO datetime with offset/Z.
since?: string,
// Only notes modified strictly before this ISO datetime with offset/Z. Use the next local midnight for a day filter.
until?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Use natural language queries with Wolfram|Alpha to get up-to-date computational results about entities in chemistry, physics, geography, history, art, astronomy, and more.

```ts
mcp__Wolfram__WolframAlpha(args: {
// Wolfram Alpha query
query: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Uses semantic search to retrieve any relevant information from Wolfram.
Always use this tool at the start of new conversations or if the topic changes to ensure you have up-to-date relevant information.
This uses semantic search, so the context argument should be written in natural language (not a search query) and contain as much detail as possible (up to 250 words).

```ts
mcp__Wolfram__WolframContext(args: {
// A detailed summary of what the user is trying to achieve or learn about.
context: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Evaluates Wolfram Language code for the user in a Wolfram Language kernel.
If a formatted result is provided as a markdown link, use that in your response instead of typing out the output.
Parse natural language input with `\[FreeformPrompt]["query"]`, which is analogous to ctrl+= input in notebooks.
Natural language input is parsed before evaluation, so it works like macro expansion.
You should ALWAYS use this natural language input to obtain things like `Quantity`, `DateObject`, `Entity`, etc.
This is a stateless kernel, so you cannot reuse definitions from previous evaluations.

```ts
mcp__Wolfram__WolframLanguageEvaluator(args: {
// The Wolfram Language code to evaluate.
code: string,
// The time constraint for the evaluation (default is 60 seconds).
timeConstraint?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Discover the WorkOS operations available through the query and mutate tools. Without arguments, returns the full index of operation names — call it once and reuse the result. Pass filter to narrow the index by keyword (operation name or description). Pass operation for that operation’s parameters and input types, or type to fully expand a specific input/enum type by name. Call whoami to see available environments and their IDs.

```ts
mcp__WorkOS__list_operations(args: {
// Case-insensitive keyword to narrow the index by operation name or description, e.g. "branding", "sso", "organization".
filter?: string,
// An operation name from the index to get its parameters and input types.
operation?: string,
// An input or enum type name (e.g. one named in an operation’s parameters) to fully expand — use this to resolve deeply nested input shapes without triggering an error.
type?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Run a mutation against your WorkOS account by name. Mutations change real data in the targeted environment. Call list_operations first to discover available operations and their parameters. Pass environment_id to choose the environment (whoami returns your available environments); when the operation declares an environmentId parameter it is applied automatically, so you don’t pass it twice. Without environment_id, operations that don’t require one run against a sandbox environment. Destructive operations (permanent deletes) and billing changes require a confirmation token — the first call returns the impact plus a single-use "confirmation_token" and does not execute; pass the token back with the same arguments to proceed.

```ts
mcp__WorkOS__mutate(args: {
// Single-use token issued by a prior call to this operation (a permanent deletion or a billing change). Pass it back with the same arguments to execute. Ignored by operations that don’t require confirmation.
confirmation_token?: string,
// The WorkOS environment to operate in (returned by whoami). Defaults to a sandbox environment. When the operation declares an environmentId parameter, this value is applied to it automatically.
environment_id?: string,
// The mutation name from list_operations, e.g. 'createOrganization'.
operation: string,
// Parameters for the operation, keyed by name (without the $). See list_operations for each operation’s parameters.
variables?: { [key: string]: any },
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Run a query against your WorkOS account by name. Call list_operations first to discover available operations and their parameters. Pass environment_id to choose the environment (whoami returns your available environments); when the operation declares an environmentId parameter it is applied automatically, so you don’t pass it twice. Without environment_id, operations that don’t require one run against a sandbox environment.

```ts
mcp__WorkOS__query(args: {
// The WorkOS environment to operate in (returned by whoami). Defaults to a sandbox environment. When the operation declares an environmentId parameter, this value is applied to it automatically.
environment_id?: string,
// The query name from list_operations, e.g. 'organizations'.
operation: string,
// Parameters for the operation, keyed by name (without the $). See list_operations for each operation’s parameters.
variables?: { [key: string]: any },
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Report a WorkOS MCP bug, missing capability, or confusing behavior to the WorkOS team. Include what you tried, what you expected, what happened, and any workaround. Send one concise report per distinct issue, even if you recovered. Omit credentials, personal data, and full request or response payloads. This sends feedback to WorkOS; it does not open a support case or fix the issue.

```ts
mcp__WorkOS__submit_feedback(args: {
// A concise issue report with expected and actual behavior. No secrets or personal data.
message: string, // minLength: 1, maxLength: 2000
// The affected MCP tool or WorkOS operation, if known.
operation?: string, // minLength: 1, maxLength: 128
}): Promise<unknown>;
```
